# The Spider and Siva
Tamil title: சிலந்தியும் சிவனும்

We have explained many times, with many citations and irrefutable evidence, that all the stories told in the Periyapuranam are imaginary, contain not even a mustard seed of truth, and were fabricated to breathe life back into Saivism. Yet certain Saivite scholars who depend upon fraudulent books such as the Periyapuranam for their livelihood never acquire either the desire to recognise truth, adopt noble qualities, live honourably in accord with the world and the times, or the courage to understand the people's outlook, cleanse the stains upon their knowledge, dispel their infatuation with antiquity, and prepare a direct path toward the refined life humanity expects.

Why? It is shame mingled with embarrassment: "After believing the Puranas to be true and teaching them to the people for so long, how can we now expose their corruption and say they are all false and must not be believed?" This embarrassment resembles the saying:

> "Even after gaining sight, the blind man, treating light as an enemy, is ashamed to discard his stick."

A man blinded by disease walks with a stick. Medicine later cures the disease and restores his sight, yet he will not release the stick he held while blind, because light itself has supposedly become his enemy. So it is with our Saivite scholars. When ignorance prevented them from knowing truth, they believed the Puranas and treated them as instruments for sharpening knowledge. Later, unable to obtain clarity by themselves, they are lovingly shown by truth-seekers, like physicians treating disease, which is false and which is true, and urged to abandon falsehood. Some acquire that clarity themselves through the passage of time. Yet they do not relinquish the Puranas that caused the darkness of ignorance, as though clear knowledge had become their incurable enemy. Whether this comes from ingrained habit or fear that the world will laugh, we do not know. We truly pity scholars afraid to acknowledge truth once known or shown to them.

A hen accustomed to scratching rubbish for food will scratch even a heaped mound of grain before eating. Likewise, scholars who have wandered through Puranic refuse seeking truth continue to scratch the same old rubbish even after being shown where truth lies. What example, or whom, can we show them? A hen may at least find an insect or worm in refuse; scholars who scratch through the Puranas cannot obtain even a millet seed of truth. Are not the Puranas instruments that conceal even the little truth they contain? To drive truth beyond the world, one need only read the Puranas. They are mixed wares of falsehood, lies, and fabrication.

Some Saivite scholars will occasionally admit this when circumstances suit them; at another time they declare that they will not even tread upon the shadow of those who call the Puranas false. We repeatedly write such investigations to rescue them from this plight of the ant caught between two burning ends.

A calf tied to a post is released but does not move, still imagining itself bound. The person who untied it must tap and drive it away. Likewise, we try to free scholars caught in the grip of the Puranas. They remain bound within what they regard as their refuge. Those who understand the baseness of the Puranas release their bonds, yet they do not leave. Some merely resemble the released calf; most seem to think, "Who is he to untie us? Who are we to leave merely because he has released the knot?"

Water poured for paddy benefits grass too. In that spirit, may explanations written for those capable and desirous of reform benefit both good and bad. This is not our only reason. A channel carries water from a distant source toward a paddy field, but the water first nourishes grass along its banks. Similarly, let work intended for the good first benefit those who are bad. Once they reform, correcting those already inclined toward goodness will be easier. Therefore we address people attached to the Puranas and challenge them: "What answer will you give?"

The Periyapuranam says an elephant and a spider worshipped Siva and received his grace. Is that true? The Puranas themselves say even human birth does not make Mahesvara's grace easy to obtain. How, then, are we to believe that an elephant and spider received it? Here is the story.

The place now called Tiruvanaikkoyil, Tiruvanaikkaval, or Tiruvanaikka was once a forest. Beneath a white-jambul tree stood a Sivalinga, which a white elephant discovered. The elephant desired to worship it. It carried water in its trunk, bathed the linga, plucked flowers, and adorned it. Thus the place acquired the name Tiruvanaikka.

A spider in the forest also saw the linga and noticed dry leaves falling upon it. To prevent this, it spun a canopy above the linga with thread from its mouth. The worshipping elephant saw the canopy, regarded it as an offence, and destroyed it. Thinking the elephant's moving trunk had accidentally disturbed it, the spider built another. The elephant destroyed that too. Enraged, the spider entered the elephant's trunk and bit it. The elephant fell, struck its trunk repeatedly, and died; the spider inside died as well. Seeing this, Siva granted the elephant salvation and blessed the spider to be born in the Chola lineage.

The spider's life entered the womb of the wife of a Chola king named Subadeva. When she was about to give birth, an astrologer said, "The child must not be born now. If it is born one naligai later, it will rule the three worlds." She ordered, "Then bind me upside down." This was done. Because the delayed child was born while she hung inverted, it had blood-red eyes. Seeing it, the mother exclaimed, "My Ko-Chengannan!" and died.

The child grew up, became king of the Chola country, went to Tiruvanaikka, and built a Siva temple there. Later he went to Tillai, provided beautiful mansions for the Brahmins, and reached Siva's feet. Such is the story in brief.

How did the elephant conceive a desire to worship a forest linga? Wild animals do not naturally desire to worship God. How did it recognise the linga as God's emblem? Who taught it that bathing the linga and adorning it with flowers constituted worship? Even humans, supposedly born specifically to worship God, cannot independently discover him or learn the method of worship. Though instructed by many over many days, they cannot comprehend God's form or proper worship. If this is the condition of reasoning humans, how can we accept as true that a non-rational animal saw a stone in a forest, identified it as Siva's emblem, understood the requisite rites, and performed them?

Some devout Saivites may say the elephant understood everything through Siva's grace. Let us set aside for their sake the settled Saiva Siddhanta conclusion that divine grace cannot be obtained except through human birth, and accept that the elephant received grace and thereby knew and worshipped Siva.

If a wild, non-rational elephant effortlessly received grace said to remain inaccessible even to reasoning humans after prolonged effort, why did it destroy the spider's canopy that protected the linga from leaves? Why did grace not enable it to understand that another creature had built the canopy for protection? Scholars ask us to believe that an elephant received enough grace to recognise a forest stone as God and worship it. Yet when we ask why that same elephant could not recognise the protective work of a spider blessed like itself, they shamelessly call us atheists.

We are expected to close our eyes and minds and believe them. We never compel them to believe us. Because compulsion is wrong, we place our investigated conclusions before the public and ask only that people consider and accept them if reason approves. Rationalists never curse, "Believe us, or you will go to hell." Submitting every claim to public judgment is the method and duty of a rationalist who moves with thought and time. May aggressive Saivite scholars at least now understand this.

The claim that the life of the spider killed inside the elephant's trunk entered the womb of the Chola king's wife Kamalavati cannot be accepted according to biology. No one but the intellectually deaf will accept something contrary to truth and nature merely because Sekkizhar said it. Sekkizhar himself explains the fate suffered by Kamalavati and her child because biological birth was obstructed.

The mother was bound upside down so that the child would not emerge naturally and birth was delayed. This caused a bodily defect: blood gathered in the child's eyes, making them red. The mother, having prevented the child from being born at the biologically proper time and having hung inverted until the time passed, suffered damage to her womb and died after giving birth. Thus acting against nature and biology needlessly killed a woman and caused a child to be born disfigured. If we ask whether this is just, perhaps they will award us another title of atheist.

Nor does Sekkizhar offer any evidence that the child, born one naligai late as the astrologer prescribed, consequently ruled three worlds. The Purana does not explain which worlds they were. Some Puranas speak of fourteen worlds, others of three. Devotees have yet to answer either which account is true or the rationalist who says both are false.

Having summoned an astrologer to mark the "auspicious" moment of birth, the Chola king should also have summoned a physician and asked whether delaying birth by one naligai would harm the mother or child. He did not. A child will be born at its proper time without an astrologer nearby, but a physician is necessary at childbirth. Even today, however poor a household may be, people arrange according to their means for a doctor or midwife. Yet this supposedly intelligent king had no such thought, nor could he, because religious books teach people not to do what is necessary and to do what is unnecessary. Thus he summoned the useless astrologer and omitted the necessary physician. How can truth that illuminates knowledge be expected from Puranas written to dim even the little intelligence people possess?

Would lovers of the Puranas, had they wished to write in harmony with truth and reason, have packed nine absurdities into one event? Consider how Sekkizhar describes Siva rewarding the elephant and spider. Each disliked what the other did, though both were equally devoted. The elephant wished to bathe and adorn the linga; the spider wished to protect that bathed and adorned linga from falling leaves.

By religious doctrine, the spider's love might even be called greater, for it sought to safeguard the linga after the elephant's worship. Yet Siva's grace was partial. The elephant received immediate liberation, the bliss beyond rebirth, and was taken to Siva at once. The more devoted spider was made a Chola prince and additionally blessed with a deformed body.

To a rationalist all this shines like a beacon as distilled falsehood. Yet lovers of the Puranas believe every account literally, proclaim it as truth, and thrill with delight. In the hope that they will at least now recognise these confusions and contradictions and gain clarity, I state that I write such investigations without departing from impartiality, and here conclude this one.

27-7-1947

---NOTES---
`சிவகதி` is translated as “salvation,” and `பிறவாப் பேரின்பம்` as “the bliss beyond rebirth,” preserving the Saivite doctrinal contrast used by the author.
`நாழிகை` is retained as naligai, a traditional Tamil unit of approximately twenty-four minutes.
The running header on scan 7 and three corrupt forms of `கீழே/தலைகீழாக` were corrected from the scans. The terminal scan proves the article date is 27-7-1947, replacing the OCR reading 21-1-1947.
